/-!
# Compact objects

Let `D` be a (pre-)triangulated category with direct sums. An object `K` of `D` is *compact*
(`CategoryTheory.IsCompactObject`, [Stacks, Tag 07LS]) when the canonical map
`⨁ i, Hom(K, E i) ⟶ Hom(K, ∐ E)` is bijective for every family `E` whose direct sum exists. The
theory of the compact objects of `D` and of the categories they generate is split into three parts:

* `StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Triangulated.CompactObjects.ThickSubcategory`: compact objects, the
  compactness map of a cofan, the thick triangulated subcategory `D_c` they span
  ([Stacks, Tag 09QH]), and the transfer of compactness along functors;
* `StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Triangulated.CompactObjects.CellStructure`: maps out of a compact
  object into a derived colimit over `ℕ` ([Stacks, Tag 094A]), and the cell structure on the
  objects of a category generated by compact objects ([Stacks, Tag 09SN]);
* `StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Triangulated.CompactObjects.CompactGenerators`: the factorization
  of
  a map from a compact object to a cell object through finitely many generators
  ([Stacks, Tag 09SP]), and compact generators: classical generators of `D_c` versus generators
  of `D` ([Stacks, Tag 09SR]).
-/
